-- Apply every pending transfer in transfer_id order.
-- A transfer that cannot be applied must leave no trace and be marked 'failed'.
DO $$
DECLARE t record;
BEGIN
  FOR t IN SELECT * FROM transfers WHERE status = 'pending' ORDER BY transfer_id LOOP
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - t.amount WHERE account_id = t.from_id;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + t.amount WHERE account_id = t.to_id;
    UPDATE transfers SET status = 'done' WHERE transfer_id = t.transfer_id;
  END LOOP;
END $$;

solution:
DO $$
DECLARE t record;
BEGIN
  FOR t IN SELECT * FROM transfers WHERE status = 'pending' ORDER BY transfer_id LOOP
    BEGIN  -- an EXCEPTION block is a savepoint around this transfer
      IF t.amount <= 0 OR t.from_id = t.to_id THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'invalid transfer %', t.transfer_id;
      END IF;
      UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - t.amount WHERE account_id = t.from_id;
      IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'no source account %', t.from_id; END IF;
      UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + t.amount WHERE account_id = t.to_id;
      IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'no target account %', t.to_id; END IF;
      UPDATE transfers SET status = 'done' WHERE transfer_id = t.transfer_id;
    EXCEPTION WHEN check_violation OR raise_exception THEN
      -- everything since the block's BEGIN is already rolled back here
      UPDATE transfers SET status = 'failed' WHERE transfer_id = t.transfer_id;
    END;
  END LOOP;
END $$;

The unit of atomicity is one transfer, so each gets its own savepoint: the inner `BEGIN ... EXCEPTION` block. An overdraft makes the debit violate `CHECK (balance >= 0)`; the handler then runs with that transfer's changes already rolled back and only records `'failed'`. The other failures do not raise anything by themselves. An UPDATE on a missing account id matches zero rows and succeeds, so without `IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION` the debit would commit and the money would disappear. A zero or negative amount, or a self-transfer, would also pass the CHECK, so they are rejected up front. Catching only `check_violation` and `raise_exception` (instead of `OTHERS`) lets real bugs, such as a typo in a column name, still fail the whole batch loudly. The loop reads pending transfers in `transfer_id` order, so a transfer can spend money delivered earlier in the batch. The DO block runs as one transaction that commits when it finishes, so either the whole batch result is saved or (on an unexpected error) nothing is.

